@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-json-merge-patch-no-new-error-type-immutable-clone-and-proto-safety
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Merge Patch: no new error type, immutable clone, and __proto__ safety"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`applyMergePatch` implements RFC 7386, a second, independent way of expressing a partial update
+alongside `applyPatch` (RFC 6902, dl-002). Unlike JSON Patch, RFC 7386 defines no failure mode: every
+JSON value is a valid merge patch, and the merge algorithm it specifies (§2) always produces a result.
+The project's existing JSON Patch code also set precedents worth either reusing or deliberately
+departing from: immutability via `structuredClone`, and safe handling of a member literally named
+`__proto__` (dl-002).
+
+## Decision
+
+- Do not add a `JsonMergePatchError` or reuse `JsonPatchError`/`JsonPointerError`: `applyMergePatch`
+  never throws. RFC 7386's algorithm is total over all JSON values - a non-object patch simply replaces
+  the target, and a non-object target being merged into is simply discarded in favour of `{}` before
+  merging proceeds - so inventing an error type would mean inventing failure modes the RFC doesn't have.
+- Guarantee immutability the same way `applyPatch` does: `structuredClone` the target once (when it is
+  an object worth preserving) and clone any patch value that is copied into the result verbatim (a
+  non-object patch, or a member value that isn't itself merged further). This keeps the two entry
+  points of the library consistent for callers that mix both styles of patch.
+- Reuse the `__proto__`-safety approach from `applyPatch`'s `addAt`: write members with
+  `Object.defineProperty` rather than plain assignment, and read a member for the recursive-merge case
+  with an explicit `hasOwnProperty` check rather than a plain property read. Plain assignment or a plain
+  read of a key named `__proto__` on a freshly-created `{}` would route through `Object.prototype`'s
+  `__proto__` accessor instead of treating it as an ordinary member name, exactly as dl-002 already
+  found for `applyPatch`'s `add`. Removal uses the plain `delete` operator, which is safe here: it only
+  ever removes an existing *own* property (one the clone or a prior `defineProperty` call created) and
+  is a no-op otherwise, so it never reaches the inherited accessor.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers cannot distinguish "malformed input" from a valid merge, because there is no such distinction
+  to make; a caller that wants to reject a patch shape it doesn't trust (e.g. refuse a top-level array)
+  must check that itself before calling `applyMergePatch`.
+- Like `applyPatch`, every call clones the whole target rather than only the touched branches; the same
+  correctness-first trade-off from dl-002 applies here, for the same reason (no stated performance
+  requirement).
+- Tests that exercise the literal member name `__proto__` must build the patch object via `JSON.parse`
+  rather than an object literal: `{ __proto__: value }` as source syntax sets the object's prototype
+  instead of creating an own enumerable property, which would silently test the wrong thing.
