@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+---
+id: task-004-implement-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## What was done
+
+Added `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` to `src/index.ts`, implementing the RFC 7386 merge algorithm:
+
+- A `patch` that is a JSON object merges into `target` member by member, recursing into members that
+  are objects on both sides, deleting members whose patch value is `null`, and otherwise overwriting.
+- A `patch` that is not a JSON object (array, string, number, boolean, or `null`) replaces `target`
+  entirely.
+- `target` is never mutated (cloned via `structuredClone`, consistent with `applyPatch`); every value
+  copied into the result is likewise cloned so the result never aliases the target or the patch.
+- Members named literally `__proto__` are read/written/deleted safely (via `hasOwnProperty` reads and
+  `Object.defineProperty` writes), reusing the approach already used by `applyPatch`'s `add`.
+
+No new error type was introduced: RFC 7386 has no failure mode, so `applyMergePatch` never throws (see
+dl-003 for the rationale, and for the `structuredClone`/`__proto__` precedents it reuses from dl-002).
+
+Testing followed red-green: added failing tests calling the not-yet-exported `applyMergePatch` first
+(confirmed the import itself failed), then implemented the function until all tests passed. Test
+coverage: all RFC 7386 Appendix A worked examples, plus immutability of the target, non-aliasing of
+both target- and patch-derived values in the result, and the `__proto__` edge case (add and remove).
+
+Updated `README.md` with the new `applyMergePatch` section, documented the same way as the existing
+`resolvePointer`/`applyPatch` entries.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm run typecheck` - passes.
+- `npm test` - 62/62 tests pass (sections added at the end of `src/index.test.ts`).
